# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **Every door reports here, and there is one of these because every door answers the same
# way.** That is the uniform shape paying for itself: logging, instrumentation and an audit
# trail are each one place only because `self.call` looks identical on every operation. Four
# return conventions and none of them could exist.
#
# It records the **attempt**, not the change. What changed is in the row; what this holds is
# who asked, for what, and what they were told — including the refusals, which are the entries
# nobody has when they need them.
#
# **Recorded after the transaction, never inside it.** An entry written inside is erased by
# the rollback it was meant to explain, so a refused or failed attempt would leave no trace at
# exactly the moment a trace matters. The cost of writing it outside is a crash between commit
# and record, which loses one entry; the cost of writing it inside is losing every entry that
# describes a failure.
module AuditLog
  # What happened, as a value. A shape, so it holds no record and compares by value.
  class Entry < Shape
    def initialize(operation:, outcome:, actor_id: nil, error: nil)
      @operation = typed(operation, String)
      @outcome = typed(outcome, Symbol)
      @actor_id = typed(actor_id, Integer, allow_nil: true)
      @error = typed(error, Symbol, allow_nil: true)
    end

    attr_reader :operation, :outcome, :actor_id, :error
  end

  # **Arguments are deliberately not recorded, and this is not an oversight.**
  #
  # An audit log is the classic place personal data leaks: it is written on every call, kept
  # longer than the rows it describes, copied into log aggregation, and forgotten by every
  # erasure request ever written. Recording `email:` here puts a person's data somewhere
  # `personal-data-is-declared-and-erasable` cannot see and nobody will delete.
  #
  # If an operation's arguments genuinely belong in the trail, that operation records them —
  # deliberately, naming the fields, having thought about erasure. The default is silence.
  def self.record(operation:, outcome:, actor: nil, error: nil)
    sink.call(
      Entry.new(
        operation: operation.to_s,
        outcome: outcome,
        actor_id: actor.respond_to?(:id) ? actor.id : nil,
        error: error,
      ),
    )
  end

  # **The default is a log line, and a log line is not an audit trail.** It works on the day
  # you install this and it is not what you want by the time anybody asks for the trail: logs
  # rotate, they are not queryable, and they are the first thing to be shipped somewhere with
  # a different retention policy. Point this at a table.
  #
  #   AuditLog.sink = ->(entry) { AuditEntryRecord.create!(**entry.to_h) }
  # **Never silent, even with no Rails loaded.** These base classes are POROs and must load
  # in a suite that never booted the framework, so the default falls back to stderr rather
  # than to nothing — a `nil` logger would make the audit trail disappear exactly where
  # nobody is watching for it, which is `nothing-fails-quietly` broken by default.
  def self.sink
    @sink ||= lambda do |entry|
      line = "audit #{entry.inspect}"

      defined?(::Rails) && ::Rails.respond_to?(:logger) ? ::Rails.logger.info(line) : warn(line)
    end
  end

  class << self
    attr_writer :sink
  end
end
